package scryer:host@1.0.0;

/// The single host-services import shared by every Scryer plugin-family world.
///
/// # Why one function
///
/// The SDK — not WIT — owns the capability set. `request` is a postcard-encoded
/// `scryer_plugin_sdk::host::PluginHostRequest` and a successful `response` is a
/// postcard-encoded `scryer_plugin_sdk::host::PluginHostResponse`, exactly the
/// bytes the core-module host ABI (`scryer_host_call`) and the PDK's `call()`
/// already exchange today. A new capability is therefore a new SDK enum variant
/// plus a new arm in the host's service layer, with no WIT revision and no
/// re-generated bindings for the families that do not use it.
///
/// That is the whole point of the shape: `scryer:subtitle`,
/// `scryer:download-client`, `scryer:notification` and every family world that
/// follows import *this* interface unchanged, and differ only in their own
/// `describe`/`process` payloads.
///
/// # Capability availability is in-band
///
/// A host that has not been configured with a service — or a service that has
/// no provider installed — does NOT surface that as `host-error`. It returns a
/// well-formed `PluginHostResponse` whose `PluginResult` is
/// `Err(PluginError { code: Unsupported, .. })`, matching the fail-closed
/// `CommandHost` semantics the core-module guests already see. `host-error` is
/// reserved for the transport itself failing, which a guest cannot recover from
/// by asking for something else.
interface services {
  /// A transport-level failure of the host-call itself.
  ///
  /// Both variants mean the guest never reached the service layer, so no
  /// `PluginHostResponse` exists to carry a typed `PluginError`.
  enum host-error {
    /// `request` was not a decodable `PluginHostRequest`, or exceeded the
    /// host's encoded-request cap.
    invalid-request,
    /// The host could not run the service or encode its response.
    failed,
  }

  /// Exchange one encoded `PluginHostRequest` for one encoded
  /// `PluginHostResponse`.
  ///
  /// Synchronous in WIT on purpose: the host implements it as an async host
  /// function, so a guest that has no async runtime of its own (the common
  /// case, and every hand-built fixture) can still call it as an ordinary
  /// import while the host awaits HTTP, archive extraction, and the rest
  /// without blocking a runtime worker.
  host-call: func(request: list<u8>) -> result<list<u8>, host-error>;
}
